    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "27c8909e_980bab52",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 995,
      "author": {
        "id": 1122323
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-05T12:32:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yep, the reason for that was in order to give you a solid place where you know the \u0027minimum hold time\u0027 is starting. i.e. if you want to reason about the amount of time you need to keep a cpu frequency level active, you need a specific place where you measure from in my opinion.\n\nI originally chose the enqueue time and the rest of the implementation just hangs off that decision. I guess you could maybe avoid updating the rq sometimes if you made the decision at dequeue but consider the timing from the enqueue time? You might not have all the necessary information at that time, but I haven\u0027t checked.\n\nThe whole thing (enqueue vs dequeue) probably depends upon the tuning interface you\u0027re considering - schedtune used a 50ms hold for all rt tasks in the ACK configuration, starting from enqueue.\n\nThe intent here is for you to be able to use this for periodic tasks to avoid cases where they wake up and cannot select the correct frequency - in which case you need to know the period and the runtime is not necessary.",
      "parentUuid": "d47fd967_8304a1c0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 995,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 995,
        "endChar": 14
      },
