    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3409fb51_080fe5b0",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1032,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291841
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-05T16:30:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think in this case we can unnecessarily boost other tasks.\n\nHere is the case, let\u0027s assume:\n\n- TaskA: enq@t0, deq@t3, util_min:400, hold: 50ms\n- TaskB: enq@t1, deq@t2, util_min:800, hold:  5ms\n\n\n\n    HoldA\n   +------------------------------------------------------//-------+\n           HoldB                                                   |\n          +----------------------+                                 |\n                                 |                                 |\n          +---------------+      + TaskA overboost                 |\n          |TaskB          |                                        |\n          |               |      +-----------------\u003e               |\n          |               |                                        |\n   +-----------------------------------------------+               +\n   |TaskA |               |                        |\n   |      |               |                        |\n   +------+---------------+------+-----------------+\n   \n   t0     t1              t2     tx                t3\n\n\n\nIn this case TaskA will end up running at 800 for longer then required.\nShould we not instead better always reset the hold timeout every time:\n\n   min_clamp \u003e READ_ONCE(rq-\u003euclamp_hold_value)\n\nand then force a boost hold evaluation once we cross the expire?\n\nThis means that in the case above we will reset to util_min:400 at the scheduling event right after tx and maybe more likely before t3.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1028,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 1032,
        "endChar": 55
      },
      "revId": "abb2d80ea13b521ac0b22cc41811c18c500cbe5d",
